# SYNOPSIS

```usage
printenv [var ...]
```

# DESCRIPTION

With no <var>, `printenv` prints every variable of its environment as `name=value`, one to a line, in the environment's order.
Otherwise it prints the value of each <var>, one to a line, and nothing for a <var> that is not set.

`printenv` takes no flags; `--` ends them.

`printenv` is sbase's, built into `sbase-box` with sbase's other commands and installed as `/boot/bin/posix/printenv`, a hard link to it.
In the POSIX namespace (a manifest's `ns=posix`, made by `/lib/ns/posix`, see namespace(6)) `/boot/bin/posix` is bound before `/boot/bin` on `/bin`, so POSIX programs find it as `/bin/printenv`.
There is no native `printenv`.
A service's environment is what its manifest's `env=` tuples give it (see svc(6)); a program it runs gets the environment it is passed.

# EXAMPLES

`tests/posix/sbasetest.c` runs it in the POSIX namespace, its manifest giving it `env=GREETING=hello`:

```text
% printenv GREETING
hello
```

# SEE ALSO

env(1), namespace(6), svc(6); sbase's page is `third_party/sbase/printenv.1` in the repository

# DIAGNOSTICS

The exit status is 0 when every <var> is set, 1 when one is not, and 2 when the output could not be written.
A flag prints the usage message and exits 1.

# BUGS

A variable whose value holds a newline prints as more than one line, with nothing to tell where it ends.
